# The exact set of changes the substrate makes to a clean TMM tree.
#
# WHY A MANIFEST AND NOT A PATCH. Four F5 files are MODIFIED (two whitelists,
# filelist, ssl.c). Their diffs are build configuration and, for ssl.c, a
# deliberate security regression -- and this repo pushes to GitHub. So what is
# recorded here is NAMES AND STATUS, not F5 source. The whitelist symbol list and
# the filelist entries are in TMM-TREE-DELTA.md; the ssl.c revert is described in
# VULNERABLE-BUILD.md and deliberately NOT reproduced anywhere.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS AT ALL. Asked "if the build VM died could we continue", the answer
# was mostly-yes with gaps that took an audit to find: harness.c existed only on
# the build box, 8 whitelist symbols were undocumented, and
# tmm-tree-callsites.patch still patched http.c/http1x.c after that tracepoint had
# been rolled back. None of those were visible from either side alone.
#
# Checked by env/scripts/bnk-check-tree-sync.sh. A tree entry missing here, or an
# entry here missing from the tree, fails the run -- the same both-ways discipline
# as TMM's own globals whitelist, which is the only thing that caught the lost map
# registration.
#
#   M  = an F5 file we modify (diff NOT stored here -- see above)
#   ?? = a file we add; every one of these must also exist in substrate/
#
# Base: tmm/tmm at e2104734a9. Regenerate with:
#   ssh <build-box> 'cd ~/code/tmm && git status --porcelain src/' | sed 's/^ *//' | sort
#
# STALE BY 18 ENTRIES, found 2026-08-21 while auditing loose ends. Every file added to the tree
# since this list was last written was missing from it: the audit trail (ls_audit.*), Ed25519
# verification (ls_sig.*, and the GENERATED ls_sig_pubkey.h), the whole ctx-builder registry
# (ls_ctx_reg*, six files), and five headers. Eighteen of the fifty-six entries.
#
# That is a direct hit on the question this file exists to answer --- "if the build VM died could
# we continue" --- and the answer had quietly become "not without redoing the last week". The
# check that would have caught it, bnk-check-tree-sync.sh, WAS reporting it on every run, under a
# DIVERGED verdict that also fired permanently for three duplicate filenames that are by design.
# A guard that is always red is a guard nobody reads, which is the exact failure this file's own
# header warns about. Both are fixed: the duplicates are recognised, and this list is current.
#
# ls_sig_pubkey.h is listed and is GENERATED --- by bnk-init-signing-key.sh from whichever key a
# build trusts. Its presence in the tree is expected; its CONTENT is machine-specific and must not
# be copied between trees, or one build would verify against another's key.
